  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 306 PM EDT THU SEP 24 2026\n\nThe long term period will be largely characterized by pleasant \nweather. On Saturday morning, a deep vertically stacked low will \nbe located off the coast of the Mid Atlantic. Meanwhile, a highly \namplified midlevel ridge will be centered over the Mississippi \nValley, with a corresponding surface high in the Great Lakes \nregion. This pattern will remain stagnant until early next week; \nthen, the aforementioned low will finally lift north into Canada, \nallowing the ridge to flatten somewhat and build east. \nAccordingly, the surface high will shift east of the forecast \narea, opening the door for the next system to approach near the \nmiddle of next week.\n\nWith a surface high to the northwest of the forecast area, winds \nwill be largely northeasterly through early next week. This will \nkeep weather cool and dry, with highs generally ranging from the \nlow to mid 70s across the area through Monday. The air will feel \ndry too, with dewpoints remaining in the 40s and 50s. Bone dry \natmospheric profiles will also support plentiful sunshine. Each \nnight, clear skies and a weak pressure gradient will allow ridge \nvalley splits to develop, with COOP MOS guidance suggesting that \nthe most sheltered hollows could see temperatures in the low 40s \nearly Sunday and Monday morning. A brief look into this years \ntemperature records shows these lows will be the coldest eastern \nKentucky has experienced since mid May! \n\nUnfortunately for fall lovers, temperatures will trend warmer by\nTuesday as the ridge expands towards our area. Highs may climb\ninto the 80s in the warmest locations, although deterministic\nguidance still shows some spread (owing to disagreements regarding\nthe strength and positioning of the ridge at this time).\nCorresponding with the breakdown of this blocking pattern, the\neastward progression of the surface high will also allow winds to\nbecome increasingly southwesterly, bringing warm and moist air\nback into the forecast area. Highs in the 80s will be likely\nacross much of the forecast area on Wednesday, and with dewpoints\nclimbing into the 60s, it will feel more humid. This increasing\nmoisture, in tandem with an approaching trough, will enable rain\nchances to finally return to the forecast on Wednesday and\nThursday.",
